WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.368 --> 00:00:07.780
SPEAKER: Like many
Google Cloud customers,

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.060
you probably use the
Global Load Balancing

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:13.810
platform to get benefits like
high availability, low latency,

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.000
and the convenience
of a single anycast

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.130
IP to front-end your capacity.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:21.310
But did you know that by adding
Cloud CDN and Cloud Armor

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:24.790
to your existing Global HTTP(S)
load balancer deployment,

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.850
you can get faster web
performance and improved web

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.170
protection?

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:32.380
In this video, we'll
focus on Cloud CDN.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:35.020
Cloud CDN runs on our
globally-distributed edge

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.960
points to reduce network
latency by caching content

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.670
closer to your users.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.490
Whenever a request is served
from the Cloud CDN cache,

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:44.590
the load balancer doesn't
need to retrieve content

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.510
from the backend infrastructure.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:48.790
This allows you to scale
seamlessly and easily

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.680
handle large spikes in demand.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.800
Since static web elements such
as images, videos, and more,

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.050
can be served from Google's
global edge instead

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:57.760
of your backend
systems, your users

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.580
can enjoy faster page loads
and a smoother web experience.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.330
Finally, Cloud CDN helps
you optimize and reduce

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.360
the cost of delivery.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.150
It keeps load off
your web servers,

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:08.680
keeping down compute usage.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.990
And content served out
of Google's edge cache

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.270
is billed at a
lower egress cost.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.760
Once you've set up an
HTTP(S) load balancer,

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.005
Cloud CDN can be enabled by
clicking a single checkbox.

00:01:19.005 --> 00:01:21.130
Let's see a quick demo on
how to do this and verify

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:22.060
it's working.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.920
In this Google Cloud
project, I already

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.110
have a global HTTP(S)
load balancer set up

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:27.490
with a backend storage bucket.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:31.000
To enable Cloud CDN to an
existing external HTTP(S) load

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.970
balancer, just click Edit
on the load balancer page

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.400
and head to the
backend configuration.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.560
Click Edit on your
backend service

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.770
and click the checkbox
to enable Cloud CDN.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:43.510
Select your cache mode.

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:45.610
By default, Cloud
CDN will apply a time

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:48.835
to live to all static content,
such as images, JavaScripts,

00:01:48.835 --> 00:01:51.320
and CSS file types.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.980
You can also choose to use
origin cache control settings

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.340
or have Cloud CDN override the
origin cache control settings

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:59.350
and apply a cache
control value regardless

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.510
of what the origin headers say.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:02.080
That's it.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.030
The CDN configuration
options are all

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:06.490
available within the same
workflow of your load balancer

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:07.510
console.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.750
Now click Update
and Update again.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:12.670
It can take up to
10 minutes to apply.

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:14.720
To validate that
Cloud CDN is working,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.710
we can look at the log lines
that Google Cloud captures.

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:19.840
To do this, you must
first generate requests

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.610
to the external load balancer.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.560
We'll head to our
frontend serving our image

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.050
from the backend storage
bucket and refresh the page

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:28.030
to generate traffic
to the load balancer.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.000
Now let's head to Cloud
loggings Log Explorer.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.620
We can filter by
our load balancer

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.740
and the associated forwarding
rule to our frontend.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:37.300
We can see our logs.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:41.200
Logs before CDN enablement show
the JSON payload status detail,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.540
"response sent by backend."

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.660
Our most recent log now
shows the status detail,

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.730
"response from cache."

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.650
Congratulations,
you've just set up

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:54.010
Cloud CDN for your external
HTTP(S) load balancer

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.040
on Google Cloud and learned
how to check for cache hits.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.990
To learn more about Cloud CDN,
check out cloud.google.com/CDN.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:05.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

